    "verdict": "REVISE",
    "summary": "The revision handles many prior cases, but failed pending alerts can still be abandoned, and the planned error logging can expose both API secrets.",
    "findings": [
      {
        "id": "REV-017",
        "severity": "high",
        "path": "PLAN.md — `check_route` order and “Дедупликация и порядок доставки”",
        "evidence": "A `pending` alert is retried only inside the fresh-anomaly branch; there is no independent pending-alert retry worker. If Telegram fails for a low fare, step 5 records that low price. With `HISTORY_WINDOW=5`, prior prices `[100,100,100,100,100]`, candidate `40`, and threshold `0.5`, retries initially occur. After repeated failed observations, the rolling history becomes `[100,100,40,40,40]`, median is `40`, so `40 < 0.5 * 40` is false. The row remains `pending` permanently and no message is sent, despite the plan saying pending sends retry naturally.",
        "fix": "Process durable `pending` alerts independently of a new anomaly decision, using a persisted payload and retry policy. Alternatively, define a pending row as a separate retryable outbox item and prevent fresh baseline changes from deciding whether it is retried."
      },
      {
        "id": "REV-018",
        "severity": "high",
        "path": "PLAN.md — token transport and network/API error logging",
        "evidence": "Travelpayouts token is planned as a request parameter, and the Telegram bot token is necessarily embedded in the Bot API URL. The plan logs network/API errors but requires no redaction. A 401/429 handled through `requests.raise_for_status()` produces an `HTTPError` whose message contains the request URL; logging that exception can write either token into systemd/journal logs, defeating the `.env` secret-handling requirement.",
        "fix": "Never log raw request URLs or exception strings that may embed them. Redact query parameter values and `/bot<TOKEN>/` path segments before logging; keep structured logs to status code, host, route ID, and sanitized error category. Add a deterministic redaction test."
      },
      {
        "id": "REV-019",
        "severity": "medium",
        "path": "PLAN.md — `build_purchase_link` fallback",
        "evidence": "When an entry has a valid price but lacks a usable `link`, the fallback URL contains only marker, origin, destination, and locale. It omits the alerted departure and return dates, so clicking it opens a generic route search rather than the itinerary whose price/date was sent. This fails the stated goal of a ticket link for the alerted fare in exactly the documented fallback case.",
        "fix": "Generate a documented date-aware Aviasales search URL from `depart_date`/`return_date`, or mark the alert as lacking a purchase link rather than presenting a generic route page as one."
      }
    ],
